From: SF2000 Linux Port <sf2000-linux@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] MIPS: sf2000: keep boot progress visible

The userspace display owner can fail before committing its first frame.  A
dark-backlight policy then makes a running kernel look like a dead handheld and
removes the only immediate physical liveness signal.

Keep the nonblocking kernel progress helpers visible.  They do not add delays;
the display service replaces inherited panel RAM with its configured frame as
soon as the guarded panel initialization succeeds.

--- a/arch/mips/kernel/setup.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/setup.c
@@ -649 +649 @@ void sf2000_visible_stage(const char *name, unsigned int pulses)
-	sf2000_backlight_set(0);
+	sf2000_backlight_set(1);
@@ -664 +664 @@ void sf2000_visible_tick(const char *name)
-	sf2000_backlight_set(0);
+	sf2000_backlight_set(1);
